<reading-list id="reading" title="My reading">
  <@item>A playbook is not a skill. It is a plain file of 8 to 25 lines (320 lines for all 22) with no frontmatter, inside the poteto-mode skill folder. The plugin manifest registers only skills/ and agents/, so the harness does not know playbooks exist: no description to match on, no slash command, no hook. The only way in is poteto-mode, which a human must type (disable-model-invocation: true). Its last section tells the model to open one file with an ordinary file read and copy the steps into the todo tool. Subagents never pick one: "The chat that receives /poteto-mode owns the playbook, the todo list, and every Task spawn."</@item>
  <@item>Choosing a playbook is the model reading one sentence. poteto-mode ends with the 22 titles, comma-separated, under "Match the task to a playbook". There is no trigger table, no keyword list and no code. The fallback, figure-it-out, is also chosen by the model.</@item>
  <@item>Three programs appear in all 22 playbooks, and none of them decides anything: watch-pr reports a PR's state through exit codes, worktree-audit.sh suggests a bucket and never deletes, log.sh appends a row. Every gate, cap and step order on this page is prompt text.</@item>
  <@item>A human is needed in few places: typing /poteto-mode, asking for a babysit or a landing, the operator's go in Multi-phase plan and the autopilots, irreversible writes, and uncommitted work in Worktree cleanup. Everything else follows "Just do it".</@item>
  <@item>The same three rules repeat across the code playbooks: reproduce or measure on the real surface first; "Inconclusive or wrong-surface is not a pass"; a change that did not help is reverted, not left in.</@item>
  <@item>One contradiction: Opening a PR says it is "Invoked at the end of every other playbook". Seven playbooks say "Run Opening a PR" as their last step, the two autopilots use it inside, and the rest end with a reply, a plan, a diagnosis or a merge.</@item>
  <@item>Worth taking for hyper: a stop predicate that pairs a target with a floor on attempts (Hillclimb); a verdict that covers a patch, checked with git patch-id, not a commit (Shipping); pin the behaviour before moving structure (Refactoring); "No decision means no prototype"; a plan whose every PR has unit, live and perf boxes with the trunk baseline measured first (Multi-phase plan).</@item>
</reading-list>

